speculative evolution or speculative  biology can be defined as a genre  focused on hypothetical scenarios in the  evolution of life  today speculative evolution is  everywhere from video games to graphic  novels to any of the hundreds of amazing  online projects that explore worlds  beyond our own  but how did we get here  interest in the biology of fictional  life wasn't always so widespread  monsters from ancient myth weren't  typically thought of to be part of an  ecosystem they just kind of existed  the origin and rise of speculative  evolution as we know it today is a  fascinating tale deeply intertwined with  scientific advancement and full of  unexpected twists  so for this special entry into the  archive i'll give you the full tour of  this genre's incredible history and  reveal the evolution of speculative  evolution  let's start by winding back the clock to  1895.  it's been almost 40 years since charles  darwin published his theory of evolution  this foundational idea that all species  gradually change over time and through  the process of natural selection become  better suited to their environment and  by the turn of the century the  scientific community and the majority of  the educated public had accepted  evolution as a fact at this time in a  small town in the english countryside  science teacher turned author h.g wells  came up with an idea for a story called  the time machine the novel follows an  intrepid english inventor on his journey  into an imagined future the novel  represented a lot of firsts for science  fiction and has been adapted into  numerous films most famously in 1960 in  both the novel and the film upon  arriving into the distant future using  his titular time machine the protagonist  realizes that darwinian evolution has  changed the english countryside beyond  recognition not only is the world now  populated with strange plant life  unfamiliar to the time traveler but  humans have evolved as well having split  into a prey species the uncanny  childlike eloy and a predator species  the secretive subterranean morlocks the  1960 film portrays the morlocks as these  weird yeti-like things an effect that  hasn't aged particularly well but their  description in the novel is more ominous  with the narrator likening their  appearance to subterranean fish their  bodies slick and bleached from millennia  spent adapting to the caverns most of  the science in this section is now  somewhat outdated but the story doesn't  end here while this first section of the  novel is most popular and the only one  covered in film adaptations the truly  groundbreaking chapters come after  the time traveler then journeys millions  of years forward and encounters a barren  version of earth lit by a dying red sun  on a ruined shoreline the time traveler  sees that giant crab-like creatures have  evolved and now keep a grim vigil over  this dying land a sight that nearly  shatters his psyche pushing even further  into the future he witnesses the final  days of earth whereupon a blood-red sea  an indescribable mass of tentacles rises  from the water a visual so beyond his  19th century comprehension it causes his  mind to collapse  fitting for a novel about time travel  this section feels impossibly ahead of  its time it doesn't read like something  written over 30 years before the  invention of sliced bread  by speculating on what life might look  like millions of years beyond his own  time wells had inadvertently opened the  door to a whole new literary genre the  genre of speculative evolution  the people of mars probably walk on  their hind legs  their barrel chested bodies covered with  a coating of down their ample skulls  would be crammed with intelligence  and their trunk-like noses indispensable  for feats of engineering  the next great frontier in speculative  evolution would emerge that same year  when astronomer percival lowell produced  an account of what he believed to be  canals on the surface of mars what we  now know to be an optical illusion  caused by streaks of dust was at the  time believed by some to be evidence  that there was complex life on the next  rock over from earth  inspired by these findings h.g wells  would publish the war of the worlds just  two years after the time machine a novel  notable for popularizing the alien  invasion story  wells tale about martians invading earth  is widely interpreted as commentary on  british imperialism yet war of the  worlds could also be considered another  founding work of speculative evolution  before the book's release wells  published numerous articles speculating  about what life on mars might look like  discussing his theories for how plant  and animal life might evolve we now know  that mars can't support life for a range  of reasons but at the time this was  reasonable scientific conjecture it's  interesting to read these articles  because they really feel like someone  gushing about a speculative world  they've created and the early ideas for  the martians and their planet wells  outlines are almost unchanged in war of  the worlds which suggests this little  project inspired the massively  influential follow-up one of the most  important sci-fi novels ever began with  the author dreaming about speculative  ecosystems  today a space conscious public avidly  consumes tons of story material about  life on other planets  in the 20th century the war of the  worlds would inspire numerous other  invasion stories which tended to echo  public perceptions of threat bolstered  by reports of supposed ufos in a rising  public interest in everything space  related science fiction stories based on  threats from the stars became a common  form of entertainment yet with a few  exceptions these aliens weren't quite  speculative in the way wells early work  was they were more things that went bump  in the night what ecosystems or  evolutionary paths created these aliens  was rarely at the forefront of these  stories they were essentially fantasy  monsters with their speculative biology  and afterthought at best in the 1950s  especially the aliens of hollywood came  to represent fears of communist  infiltration with the paranoia that  agents of the enemy were everywhere  manifesting as literal aliens in  disguise  through the years there have been  frequent rumors that martians disguised  as earthmen walk amongst us  these stories are perhaps strengthened  by the thousands of reports of  unidentified flying objects passing in  an endless procession across our skies  while the initial speculative intrigue  that inspired wells to write war of the  worlds in the first place had largely  been forgotten by the midpoint of the  20th century some lesser-known  speculative projects did emerge during  this time in 1957 animation director  ward kimball got funding from walt  disney to create mars and beyond an  animated special most famous for  depicting what conditions on mars might  be like given sufficient water with  advice from astronomer dr e c silfer  kimball and his team brought to life an  impressively alien array of speculative  life forms like trilobite creatures that  can endure martian dust storms  fast-growing carnivorous plant life and  a slew of predators including one that  incapacitates prey with poisonous gas  and another that stuns with ultrasonic  waves  perhaps most notable of all one sequence  also speculates on life based on  entirely new chemical patterns imagining  organisms based on silicon instead of  carbon providing a range of surreal new  forms  while mars and beyond would be largely  forgotten over the following decades the  piece stands as remarkably innovative by  the standards of its time  throughout the same period running  parallel to the wave of aliens were  stories which followed in the footsteps  of the time machine and explored future  and alternate evolution on earth in 1930  author olaf stapleton released the novel  last and first men which chronicles a  speculative future of humanity across 2  billion years in 18 human species the  book is somewhat unwieldy but it did  anticipate the invention of genetic  engineering  the next major work that didn't involve  aliens came in 1961  with the account of naturalist harold  stumpke's expedition to an island chain  in the south pacific  the report describes in detail a  previously unknown order of mammals  known as the snouters these mammals are  distinguished by the nasarium a  structure derived from their nose that  has been adapted for all sorts of  purposes  reportedly some used their noses for  walking others for ensnaring prey and  still others for rudimentary flight if  all this sounds too fantastical to be  real that's because it was a total  fabrication harold stumpke wasn't a real  person but the pseudonym of the real  author gerald steiner a zoologist who  wrote and published his book on the  snouters to prank his colleagues yet the  snouters mark several important firsts  in the history of speculative evolution  containing a detailed taxonomic tree and  a surprising amount of thought put into  the niches each snouter fills in fact  the tone itself is so dry and scholarly  that it successfully mimics a scientific  work and has become something of a  favorite prank among zoologists with  several scientists and publishers having  written about the snowders since as if  they were real to keep the hoax alive  and when scientists actually discovered  a new species of rodent with a uniquely  large nose in 2015 they officially named  the animal sulawesi snouter in honor of  steiner's fictitious creatures  while the snouters project was limited  to a single island archipelago two  decades later in 1981 author dougal  dixon would forever change the genre  with his book after man set 50 million  years from now after man explored the  biology of speculative life-forms that  have evolved following a mass extinction  to this day after man is recognized as  the first truly large-scale speculative  evolution project involving an entire  world with a vast array of species  after man also marks the first time the  terms speculative evolution and  speculative biology were used in writing  to describe a work  some of my favorite creatures from this  project include the rabux which are  rabbits that have evolved to fill the  grazing niche of deer or the enormous  vortex filter feeders evolved from  penguins that fill the role left behind  by whales perhaps the most famous  creature from after man is the night  stalker a nightmarish fully terrestrial  descendant of the humble bat dixon  himself has stated the final chapters of  wells's the time machine were his  primary influence in creating after man  and the book can be considered a rebirth  of the genre the volume enjoyed  widespread success across the globe and  was even made into a stop-motion  animated adaptation in japan although  the project was never translated  following up on after man's success  dixon would create the new dinosaurs a  book that explored a hypothetical  alternate earth where the cretaceous  paleogene extinction event never  occurred  leading non-avian dinosaurs to evolve  into the present day the new dinosaurs  is one of the first entries in the  alternate timeline sub-genre of  speculative evolution  yet things became more complicated with  dixon's third book the bizarre man after  man which explores the evolution of  future humans various higher-ups altered  the book heavily during development and  dixon himself was reluctant to be  involved in the final version of the  project due to its lack of scientific  detail it did give us this famous image  which has been turned into a meme but  following the book's publication no  projects like it were released for some  time  in the early 90s a different kind of  speculative work would arrive on the  scene in the form of expedition created  by artist and writer wayne barlow  expeditions surveyed the alien ecosystem  of the fictional planet darwin 4 with  the same level of detail that dixon used  when exploring future timelines  the creatures of darwin 4 were designed  to be truly alien with barlow having  grown dissatisfied with the common  science fiction trope of alien life  being similar to life on earth  at the time of the book's publication  the kinds of aliens you'd see on tv were  mainly actors with things glued to their  foreheads yet the organisms of darwin 4  felt authentically like they'd emerge  from a different evolutionary line with  none of them possessing eyes hair or  external ears in the most intelligent  aliens of darwin 4 the bizarre floating  eosapians break the trend of aliens with  human-like intelligence needing to  appear humanoid  following the success of expedition the  book was adapted in 2005 into a cgi tv  special for the discovery channel titled  alien planet barlow served as the design  consultant and one of the executive  producers of the adaptation  throughout the early 2000s  computer-generated television specials  became a surprising haven for  speculative works including charles  foley's dragons of fantasy made real  which explored the hypothetical  evolution of a lost order of dragons  joanna adams's the future is wild also  came out around this time which explores  future ecosystems in a nature  documentary format dougal dixon was  actually one of the scientists who  helped develop the project and assisted  in designing many of the life forms the  future is wild is a speculative project  that remains unparalleled in terms of  scale and ambition and there's never  really been anything like it produced  since  during the latter 2010s speculative  evolution had a significant if often  overlooked impact on popular culture  dixon's the new dinosaurs heavily  influenced the direction of peter  jackson's king kong  even james cameron's avatar which  remains one of if not the most  successful film of all time owes heavy  inspiration to art wayne barlow created  for the movie  at the same time that speculative  evolution was influencing mainstream  media there was also an explosion of  availability in online projects and  leading the charge for early internet  speculative works was one cm koziman  born in turkey the young artist and  writer released the book all tomorrows  onto the internet in 2006  a fascinating and disturbing narrative  exploring future human evolution both  through genetic engineering and natural  means the book is something to behold at  once both nihilistic and strangely  hopeful taking inspiration from works  like last and first men all tomorrows  steadily grew an online following around  the same time kozaman also released  updates for a story called snyad a world  building project exploring the biology  of aliens on a fictional exoplanet  influenced by works like barlow's  expedition the sheer number of invented  creatures and lineages makes nyad one of  the most biologically diverse fictional  worlds a few years later kozaman would  release all yesterdays with artist john  conaway and paleontologist darin naish a  book that speculated on how dinosaurs  might have looked and behaved this  speculation actually helped inspire  projects like prehistoric planet the  book also contained a section titled all  todays which explored how aliens might  reconstruct earth animals based on the  fossil record with no knowledge of their  biology  like species filling niches of a new  environment speculative evolution would  become a bigger and more complicated  genre than ever thanks to online  possibilities the early works of artist  alex reese also hit the internet around  this time showcasing stunning depictions  of alien life in particular alex reese  would explore the development of an  intelligent hexapod species called the  buren creating one of the most  fascinating unbelievable alien cultures  across fiction leading up to the modern  day speculative evolution became more  accessible than ever before with a  growing community of people inspired by  the genre able to connect over vast  distances and today speculative  evolution is in the middle of a kind of  renaissance creators like christian  klein are now self-publishing entire  speculative books like the teeming  universe dylan beta helped kick-start a  new sub-genre with serena which has  popularized the idea of a seed world a  concept that involves leaving a few  species on an empty planet and following  their evolutionary path artist roxy  valdez has continued the tradition of  alternate timeline speculative evolution  with their frog centric amphitera  project the sheer number of speculative  projects being developed online right  now is amazing and the future of the  genre is looking bright  the history of speculative evolution is  a long and interesting road i couldn't  cover everything especially since  exactly what can be defined as  speculative evolution is somewhat  nebulous but i hope this offered a  helpful overview of the major milestones  and speaking of milestones this video is  releasing on the week of my channel's  one year anniversary whether you're a  long time fan or a first time viewer it  means so much that you've stopped by and  helped make this channel possible i've  explored many of the projects mentioned  here in greater detail and i have lots  of exciting things planned for the  future  and as always thanks for watching  if you enjoyed this entry please lend  your support by liking subscribing and  hitting the notification icon to stay up  to date on all things curious see you in  the next video  foreign

